From c5c9fadf0a71e6fdf5b8c1e2f5b08bd48f49fae0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Boyd <danieljboyd@icloud.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:14:31 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] xpbs: offer copied files as text/uri-list and
 x-special/gnome-copied-files

When the general pasteboard holds file names (NSFilenamesPboardType),
xpbs offered them on CLIPBOARD only as FILE_NAME, with the last file as
a localhost URI.  File managers don't read FILE_NAME, so files copied in
a GNUstep application could not be pasted into them: they paste copied
files from x-special/gnome-copied-files (GNOME Files, Nemo, Caja, Thunar)
or text/uri-list (GNOME Files 4x, Dolphin).

xpbs now also offers, for file names:
- text/uri-list: every file as a file URI, one per line, each line
  ending in CRLF (RFC 2483), unless an NSURLPboardType already supplies
  text/uri-list, which then keeps answering it as before;
- x-special/gnome-copied-files: "copy" and then a file URI per line,
  the GNOME convention the GTK file managers read (a copy, never a cut).
FILE_NAME is answered as before.

Testing: Tests/x11/xpbscopiedfiles.m puts two files (one with a space
in its name) on the general pasteboard, waits for gpbs to take
CLIPBOARD, then plays the pasting client from its own X connection: it
asks for TARGETS, text/uri-list and x-special/gnome-copied-files with
XConvertSelection and checks the lists and the exact bytes, and checks
that copied text still answers UTF8_STRING and offers neither file
type.  It needs the gpbs from this tree running (it skips if no gpbs
takes CLIPBOARD).  Against the gpbs built from this tree the nine checks
pass; against an unpatched gpbs the file checks fail.  By hand: copy a
file in a GNUstep app, paste in GNOME Files.

AI assistance: this change, its test and this description were written
with Claude (Anthropic's Claude Code).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
